The routine atn-s computes the arctangent of a ratio, in whole degrees, by a polynomial of two terms.

Input: n from -999 to 999, standing for the ratio n / 100. Result: atan(n / 100) in whole degrees, rounded,
from -84 to 84.

Call: load n into the accumulator and JMP 26. It returns with the result in the accumulator.
Cells 26-98 (73 cells). It writes cell 99 and, of its own cells, 83-90, 98; nothing else.
Cost: at most 7909 instructions a call, 6729.0 on average over the domain, counted from the calling JMP to the
return jump. Worst error: 0.694 degrees from the exact value, at n = -780; 282 of the 1,999 inputs are not the
correctly rounded value.
To move the routine down by d cells, subtract d from every address in its instructions that points into
cells 26-98; addresses 00 and 99 stay as they are. It holds no address bases.

